      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Difference between `COUNT(*)`, `COUNT(col_name)` and `COUNT(any_value)`?\n→ `COUNT(*)` counts all the rows doesnt care whether its null or not null. Same with group by as well\n   `COUNT(col_name)` counts only non-null value from that column\n   `COUNT(any_value)` counts all the rows, same as COUNT(*)\n\n2. `NULL` values\n→ Observations:\n        1. With operators (+, <, !=...): The result would always be `NULL`.\n        2. Sorting: NULL values are treated as the smallest among all types of values so after ascending sorting they would appear at the top.\n        3. GroupBy: NULL values are treated as a separate group and are not included in any group that contains non-NULL values.\n    \n    How to fill missing values → SELECT COALESCE(col_name, fill_value) FROM table_name\n        This will not work - COALESCE(col_name, AVG(col_name)) - You need to use correlated subqueries\n            SELECT\n                name,\n                branch,\n                COALESCE(marks, (SELECT AVG(marks)\n                                        FROM table_name AS T2\n                                        WHERE T1.branch = T2.branch))        \n             FROM table_name AS T2;\n",
      "fontSize": 16.326830821750868,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Difference between `COUNT(*)`, `COUNT(col_name)` and `COUNT(any_value)`?\n→ `COUNT(*)` counts all the rows doesnt care whether its null or not null. Same with group by as well\n   `COUNT(col_name)` counts only non-null value from that column\n   `COUNT(any_value)` counts all the rows, same as COUNT(*)\n\n2. `NULL` values\n→ Observations:\n        1. With operators (+, <, !=...): The result would always be `NULL`.\n        2. Sorting: NULL values are treated as the smallest among all types of values so after ascending sorting they would appear at the top.\n        3. GroupBy: NULL values are treated as a separate group and are not included in any group that contains non-NULL values.\n    \n    How to fill missing values → SELECT COALESCE(col_name, fill_value) FROM table_name\n        This will not work - COALESCE(col_name, AVG(col_name)) - You need to use correlated subqueries\n            SELECT\n                name,\n                branch,\n                COALESCE(marks, (SELECT AVG(marks)\n                                        FROM table_name AS T2\n                                        WHERE T1.branch = T2.branch))        \n             FROM table_name AS T2;\n",
